--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party material redistributed in this repository
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This file covers third-party material stored in THIS REPOSITORY. Musicata's Rust
and npm dependencies are fetched at build time and are not checked in here; their
licenses are recorded in Cargo.lock / package-lock.json and in the per-dependency
comments in each crate's Cargo.toml.

They ARE, however, compiled into every Musicata binary — the server links its Rust
dependency tree statically and embeds the built web app — so the attribution those
licenses require travels with the release tarball and the container images in
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md. Regenerate it with `node scripts/gen-third-party-notices.mjs`
after changing dependencies.

Two third-party works are downloaded at runtime rather than redistributed, and so
carry no obligation here:

  - The PANNs CNN14 ONNX model used by musicata-ml, fetched on first run
    (see docs/musicata-ml.md). The model is published under the MIT license; it is a
    third-party ONNX export of PANNs (Kong et al.), whose original release is
    Apache-2.0.

  - AutoEq headphone correction presets, fetched from the AutoEq project when a user
    picks their headphone model in the equalizer (see docs/dsp.md). The request is made
    by the server on the user's behalf and relayed straight to that user's browser
    (crates/musicata-server/src/proxy.rs): nothing is written to disk or to the
    database, so no Musicata instance accumulates a copy of the corpus, and a preset
    comes to rest only in the profile the user chooses to save. AutoEq's own code is
    MIT (c) 2018 Jaakko Pasanen, but its published results derive from headphone
    measurements contributed by third parties (oratory1990, Crinacle, Rtings and
    others), each publishing under its own terms — some non-commercial. Do not bundle
    them into a fork's binary, and do not add a persistent cache, without first
    checking the terms of the measurement sources they derive from.

